/-!
# Cohen rings

A *Cohen ring* is a complete discrete valuation ring whose uniformizer is a
prime number. The ring of `p`-adic integers `ℤ_p` is a canonical example,
witnessing that the notion is non-vacuous.
-/

@[expose] public section

/-- A *Cohen ring* is a complete discrete valuation ring whose uniformizer is
a (rational) prime number `p`. Concretely, `R` is a domain, it is a discrete
valuation ring, it is complete for the maximal-ideal-adic topology, and there
is a prime number `p ∈ ℕ` such that the maximal ideal of `R` is generated by
the image of `p` in `R`.

This is a class extending `IsDiscreteValuationRing` and `IsAdicComplete` for the maximal ideal, so
that both the valuation-theoretic and the completeness API of a Cohen ring are available to
instance search without unfolding the definition, and only the arithmetic condition on the
uniformizer is a field of its own. -/
@[stacks_fixed 0327, ega "0 19.8.4" "partial", sga "7 I 0.5.3"]
class IsCohenRing (R : Type*) [CommRing R] [IsDomain R] : Prop extends
    IsDiscreteValuationRing R, IsAdicComplete (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R) R where
  /-- The uniformizer of `R` is a prime number `p`: there exists a prime
  natural number `p` such that the maximal ideal of `R` equals the principal
  ideal generated by the image of `p` in `R`. -/
  exists_prime_maximalIdeal_eq_span : ∃ p : ℕ, p.Prime ∧
    IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R = Ideal.span {(p : R)}

namespace IsCohenRing

variable {R : Type*} [CommRing R] [IsDomain R]

/-- A Cohen ring is a discrete valuation ring. -/
theorem isDVR (h : IsCohenRing R) : IsDiscreteValuationRing R := h.toIsDiscreteValuationRing

/-- A Cohen ring is complete with respect to its maximal-ideal-adic topology. -/
theorem isAdicComplete (h : IsCohenRing R) :
    IsAdicComplete (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R) R := h.toIsAdicComplete

/-- The uniformizer of a Cohen ring is a prime number `p`: there exists a prime natural number `p`
such that the maximal ideal equals the principal ideal generated by the image of `p`. -/
theorem uniformizer_prime (h : IsCohenRing R) : ∃ p : ℕ, p.Prime ∧
    IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R = Ideal.span {(p : R)} :=
  h.exists_prime_maximalIdeal_eq_span

end IsCohenRing

/-- The ring of `p`-adic integers `ℤ_p` is a Cohen ring, witnessing that the
notion of Cohen ring is non-vacuous. -/
theorem padicInt_isCohenRing (p : ℕ) [hp : Fact p.Prime] :
    IsCohenRing (PadicInt p) where
  exists_prime_maximalIdeal_eq_span := ⟨p, hp.out, PadicInt.maximalIdeal_eq_span_p⟩

/-!
# Cohen rings exist for every field of positive characteristic

Proof of `IsCohenRing.exists_residueField_ringEquiv`: for a prime number `p` and a field `k` of
characteristic `p` there is a Cohen ring `Λ` with `maximalIdeal Λ = pΛ` and `Λ/pΛ ≅ k`.

The ring `ℤ_p` of `p`-adic integers is a Cohen ring for `𝔽_p`, and `k` is an extension of
`𝔽_p = ResidueField ℤ_p`, so Lemma 03C3 produces a flat local `ℤ_p`-algebra `R` with `𝔪_R = p R`
and `ResidueField R ≅ k`. The Cohen ring is the completion `Λ = AdicCompletion (𝔪_R) R`: it is
Noetherian because `R/𝔪_R` is a field and `𝔪_R = (p)` is finitely generated (Lemma 05GH), it is
local and `𝔪_R`-adically complete with `𝔪_Λ = 𝔪_R Λ = pΛ` and `ResidueField Λ ≅ ResidueField R ≅ k`
(Lemma 05GG), and `p` is a nonzerodivisor in `Λ` because it is one in `R`, flatness over `ℤ_p`
carrying the regularity of `p` up. A local ring separated for the adic topology of a principal
maximal ideal `(π)` with `π` a nonzerodivisor is a domain — every nonzero element is a unit times a
power of `π` — so `Λ` is a Noetherian local domain with nonzero principal maximal ideal, hence a
discrete valuation ring by Lemma 00PD, hence a Cohen ring.

## Main results

* `Stacks.RingTheory.CohenRingExistence.solution`: the proof of
  `IsCohenRing.exists_residueField_ringEquiv`.

## Implementation notes

The source deduces that `p` is a nonzerodivisor in `Λ` from flatness of `ℤ_p → Λ`, which it gets
from flatness of `ℤ/p^n → Λ/p^nΛ = R/p^nR` by Lemma 0523. Regularity of `p` in `Λ` is proved here
directly from regularity of `p` in `R`, which is `IsSMulRegular.of_flat` applied to the flat
`ℤ_p`-algebra `R`: an element of `Λ = lim R/(p^n)` killed by `p` has every component killed by
`p`, so lies in every `p^nΛ` and vanishes. The intermediate flatness of `ℤ_p → Λ` is not needed,
and neither is the dimension estimate of Lemma 00KW, item (4) of Lemma 00PD taking the place of
its item (3).

Everything the source states for `Λ` alone is proved here for an arbitrary ring, that being the
generality it has. `isSMulRegular_algebraMap_adicCompletion` transports a nonzerodivisor `π` to
the `(π)`-adic completion; `exists_isUnit_mul_pow_of_maximalIdeal_eq_span` is the factorization
`x = u π ^ n` in a local ring separated for the adic topology of `𝔪 = (π)`, which needs neither
`π` regular nor the ring a domain and is the reason it *is* a domain
(`isDomain_of_isSMulRegular_of_maximalIdeal_eq_span`); and
`exists_isDomain_isCohenRing_of_maximalIdeal_eq_span` assembles those with Lemma 00PD into the
criterion that a Noetherian local ring complete for a maximal ideal generated by a rational prime
that is a nonzerodivisor is a Cohen ring. The last is stated as `∃ _ : IsDomain S, IsCohenRing S`
because `IsCohenRing` takes `[IsDomain S]` as an instance argument, as item (1) of Lemma 00PD
does. The local structure of the completion — local ring, maximal ideal, completeness and residue
field — is Mathlib's `AdicCompletion.isLocalRing_of_fg`,
`AdicCompletion.maximalIdeal_eq_map_of_fg`, `AdicCompletion.isAdicComplete_of_fg` and
`AdicCompletion.residueField_map_bijective_of_fg`, which need only that `𝔪_R` is finitely
generated.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0328](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0328)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 03C3](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03C3)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 05GG](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05GG)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 05GH](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05GH)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 00PD](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00PD)
-/

@[expose] public section

namespace Stacks.RingTheory.CohenRingExistence

open IsLocalRing

/-- If `π` is a nonzerodivisor of `R` and `I = (π)`, then the image of `π` in the `I`-adic
completion of `R` is again a nonzerodivisor: an element of `lim R/(π ^ n)` killed by `π` has its
`(n + 1)`-st component represented by some `a` with `π a ∈ (π ^ (n + 1))`, so `a ∈ (π ^ n)` and
its `n`-th component already vanishes. -/
private theorem isSMulRegular_algebraMap_adicCompletion {R : Type*} [CommRing R] {I : Ideal R}
    {π : R} (hI : I = Ideal.span {π}) (hπ : IsSMulRegular R π) :
    IsSMulRegular (AdicCompletion I R) (algebraMap R (AdicCompletion I R) π) := by
  refine IsSMulRegular.of_right_eq_zero_of_smul fun z hz => ?_
  rw [smul_eq_mul] at hz
  ext n
  obtain ⟨a, ha⟩ :=
    Submodule.Quotient.mk_surjective (I ^ (n + 1) • ⊤ : Submodule R R) (z.val (n + 1))
  have hmk : (algebraMap R (AdicCompletion I R) π).val (n + 1)
      = Submodule.Quotient.mk (p := (I ^ (n + 1) • ⊤ : Submodule R R)) π := rfl
  have hdvd : a ∈ Ideal.span {π ^ n} := by
    have hzero : (algebraMap R (AdicCompletion I R) π * z).val (n + 1) = 0 := by rw [hz]; rfl
    rw [AdicCompletion.val_mul, hmk, ← ha] at hzero
    rw [← Module.mul_mem_span_pow_succ_iff hπ, ← Module.pow_smul_top_eq_span_pow hI,
      ← Submodule.Quotient.mk_eq_zero]
    simpa using hzero
  have hval : z.val n = Submodule.Quotient.mk (p := (I ^ n • ⊤ : Submodule R R)) a := by
    rw [← z.2 (Nat.le_succ n), ← ha]
    exact Submodule.factor_mk _ _
  have : z.val n = 0 := by
    rw [hval, Submodule.Quotient.mk_eq_zero, Module.pow_smul_top_eq_span_pow hI]
    exact hdvd
  exact this

/-- **Factorization by a uniformizer.** In a local ring separated for the adic topology of its
maximal ideal `𝔪 = (π)`, every nonzero `x` is `u π ^ n` for a unit `u`: separatedness provides a
least `n + 1` with `x ∉ (π ^ (n + 1))`, and then `x = c π ^ n` with `c ∉ 𝔪`. -/
private theorem exists_isUnit_mul_pow_of_maximalIdeal_eq_span {S : Type*} [CommRing S]
    [IsLocalRing S] [IsHausdorff (maximalIdeal S) S] {π : S}
    (hmax : maximalIdeal S = Ideal.span {π}) {x : S} (hx : x ≠ 0) :
    ∃ (n : ℕ) (u : Sˣ), x = u * π ^ n := by
  classical
  have hex : ∃ n : ℕ, x ∉ Ideal.span {π ^ n} := by
    by_contra! h
    exact hx (IsHausdorff.haus ‹IsHausdorff (maximalIdeal S) S› x fun n => by
      rw [SModEq.zero, Module.pow_smul_top_eq_span_pow hmax]
      exact h n)
  obtain ⟨n, hn⟩ : ∃ n, Nat.find hex = n + 1 :=
    Nat.exists_eq_add_one_of_ne_zero fun h => Nat.find_spec hex (by simp [h])
  have hmem : x ∈ Ideal.span {π ^ n} := not_not.mp (Nat.find_min hex (by omega))
  obtain ⟨c, hc⟩ := Ideal.mem_span_singleton'.mp hmem
  have hcu : IsUnit c := by
    refine notMem_maximalIdeal.mp fun hcm => Nat.find_spec hex ?_
    rw [hmax, Ideal.mem_span_singleton'] at hcm
    obtain ⟨d, hd⟩ := hcm
    rw [hn]
    exact Ideal.mem_span_singleton'.mpr ⟨d, by rw [← hc, ← hd]; ring⟩
  exact ⟨n, hcu.unit, by rw [hcu.unit_spec, ← hc]⟩

/-- A local ring separated for the adic topology of its maximal ideal `(π)`, with `π` a
nonzerodivisor, is a domain: factoring two nonzero elements as `u π ^ n` and `v π ^ m` turns
`u π ^ n · v π ^ m = 0` into `u v = 0` by regularity of `π ^ (n + m)`, and `u v` is a unit. -/
private theorem isDomain_of_isSMulRegular_of_maximalIdeal_eq_span {S : Type*} [CommRing S]
    [IsLocalRing S] [IsHausdorff (maximalIdeal S) S] {π : S}
    (hmax : maximalIdeal S = Ideal.span {π}) (hreg : IsSMulRegular S π) : IsDomain S := by
  have : NoZeroDivisors S := ⟨fun {a b} hab => by
    by_contra hcon
    obtain ⟨ha, hb⟩ := not_or.mp hcon
    obtain ⟨n, u, rfl⟩ := exists_isUnit_mul_pow_of_maximalIdeal_eq_span hmax ha
    obtain ⟨m, v, rfl⟩ := exists_isUnit_mul_pow_of_maximalIdeal_eq_span hmax hb
    have h0 : (u : S) * v = 0 := hreg.pow (n + m) (by
      simp only [smul_eq_mul, mul_zero, pow_add]
      linear_combination hab)
    exact zero_ne_one (isUnit_zero_iff.mp (h0 ▸ (Units.isUnit u).mul (Units.isUnit v)))⟩
  exact NoZeroDivisors.to_isDomain S

/-- **Criterion for a Cohen ring.** A Noetherian local ring `S`, complete for the adic topology of
its maximal ideal, whose maximal ideal is generated by a prime number `p` that is a nonzerodivisor,
is a Cohen ring: it is a domain, and a Noetherian local domain with nonzero principal maximal ideal
is a discrete valuation ring by item (4) of Lemma 00PD. -/
private theorem exists_isDomain_isCohenRing_of_maximalIdeal_eq_span {S : Type*} [CommRing S]
    [IsLocalRing S] [IsNoetherianRing S] [IsAdicComplete (maximalIdeal S) S] {p : ℕ}
    (hp : p.Prime) (hmax : maximalIdeal S = Ideal.span {(p : S)})
    (hreg : IsSMulRegular S (p : S)) : ∃ _ : IsDomain S, IsCohenRing S := by
  have hdom : IsDomain S := isDomain_of_isSMulRegular_of_maximalIdeal_eq_span hmax hreg
  have hne : maximalIdeal S ≠ ⊥ := by
    rw [hmax, ne_eq, Ideal.span_singleton_eq_bot]
    exact fun h => not_subsingleton S (h ▸ hreg).subsingleton
  have hitem : ∃ _ : IsLocalRing S, IsNoetherianRing S ∧ IsDomain S ∧
      (maximalIdeal S).IsPrincipal ∧ maximalIdeal S ≠ ⊥ :=
    ⟨‹IsLocalRing S›, ‹IsNoetherianRing S›, hdom, ⟨_, hmax⟩, hne⟩
  obtain ⟨_, hdvr⟩ := ((isDiscreteValuationRing_tfae S).out 4 1).mp hitem
  exact ⟨hdom, { hdvr, ‹IsAdicComplete (maximalIdeal S) S› with
    exists_prime_maximalIdeal_eq_span := ⟨p, hp, hmax⟩ }⟩



/-- For a prime number `p` and a field `k` of characteristic `p` there is a Cohen ring `Λ` with
`maximalIdeal Λ = pΛ` and residue field isomorphic to `k`: the adic completion of a flat local
`ℤ_p`-algebra `R` with `𝔪_R = pR` and residue field `k`, which Lemma 03C3 provides. -/
@[ega "0 19.8.6" "(ii), special case"]
public theorem solution (p : ℕ) (hp : p.Prime) (k : Type u) [Field k] [CharP k p] :
    ∃ (Λ : Type u) (_ : CommRing Λ) (_ : IsDomain Λ) (_ : IsCohenRing Λ),
      maximalIdeal Λ = Ideal.span {(p : Λ)} ∧ Nonempty (ResidueField Λ ≃+* k) := by
  have : Fact p.Prime := ⟨hp⟩
  let _ : Algebra (ResidueField ℤ_[p]) k :=
    ((ZMod.castHom (dvd_refl p) k).comp (PadicInt.residueField (p := p)).toRingHom).toAlgebra
  obtain ⟨R, _, _, _, _, hflat, hmap, ⟨e⟩⟩ :=
    IsLocalRing.exists_flat_residueField_algEquiv ℤ_[p] k
  have hmR : maximalIdeal R = Ideal.span {(p : R)} := by
    rw [← hmap, PadicInt.maximalIdeal_eq_span_p, Ideal.map_span]
    simp
  have hfg : (maximalIdeal R).FG := by rw [hmR]; exact ⟨{(p : R)}, by simp⟩
  have hregR : IsSMulRegular R (p : R) := by
    have h : IsSMulRegular R (algebraMap ℤ_[p] R (p : ℤ_[p])) :=
      .of_flat (.of_ne_zero (by exact_mod_cast hp.ne_zero : (p : ℤ_[p]) ≠ 0))
    simpa using h
  have hNoeth : IsNoetherianRing (AdicCompletion (maximalIdeal R) R) :=
    (completion_noetherian R (maximalIdeal R)
      (inferInstanceAs (IsNoetherianRing (ResidueField R))) hfg).1
  have hloc : IsLocalRing (AdicCompletion (maximalIdeal R) R) :=
    AdicCompletion.isLocalRing_of_fg hfg
  have hcompl : IsAdicComplete (maximalIdeal (AdicCompletion (maximalIdeal R) R))
      (AdicCompletion (maximalIdeal R) R) := AdicCompletion.isAdicComplete_of_fg hfg
  have hmaxΛ : maximalIdeal (AdicCompletion (maximalIdeal R) R)
      = Ideal.span {(p : AdicCompletion (maximalIdeal R) R)} := by
    rw [AdicCompletion.maximalIdeal_eq_map_of_fg hfg, hmR, Ideal.map_span]
    simp
  obtain ⟨hdom, hcohen⟩ := exists_isDomain_isCohenRing_of_maximalIdeal_eq_span hp hmaxΛ
    (by simpa using isSMulRegular_algebraMap_adicCompletion hmR hregR)
  refine ⟨AdicCompletion (maximalIdeal R) R, inferInstance, hdom, hcohen, hmaxΛ, ⟨?_⟩⟩
  exact (RingEquiv.ofBijective _ (AdicCompletion.residueField_map_bijective_of_fg hfg)).symm.trans
    e.toRingEquiv

end Stacks.RingTheory.CohenRingExistence

end

/-!
# Cohen rings exist for every field of positive characteristic

Let `p` be a prime number and let `k` be a field of characteristic `p`. Then there is a Cohen
ring `Λ` — a complete discrete valuation ring whose uniformizer is a prime number, Stacks 0327 —
whose uniformizer is that same `p` and with `Λ/pΛ ≅ k`. This is what makes the mixed
characteristic half of the Cohen structure theorem (Stacks 032A) unconditional: a coefficient
ring (Stacks 0326) of a complete local ring `(R, 𝔪)` in which no power of `p` vanishes has to be
a Cohen ring with residue field `R/𝔪`, and this lemma is what produces one.

For `k = 𝔽_p` one may take `Λ = ℤ_p`, and for `k` perfect the ring `W(k)` of `p`-typical Witt
vectors; the content of the lemma is a general, possibly imperfect, `k`, for which no functorial
construction is available and `Λ` is unique only up to isomorphism.

The source's proof. `ℤ_p` is a Cohen ring for `𝔽_p`. For general `k`, choose a flat local ring
map `ℤ_p → R` with `𝔪_R = pR` and `R/pR = k` (Lemma 03C3) and let `Λ = R^∧` be its completion:
`Λ` is Noetherian (Lemma 05GH) and is local and complete with maximal ideal `(p)` because
`Λ/pΛ = R/pR` is a field (Lemma 05GG), and `ℤ_p → Λ` is flat because every
`ℤ/p^nℤ → Λ/p^nΛ = R/p^nR` is (Lemma 0523). Hence `p` is a nonzerodivisor in `Λ`, so
`dim Λ ≥ 1` (Lemma 00KW) and `Λ` is regular of dimension `1`, i.e. a discrete valuation ring
(Lemma 00PD).

## Main results

* `IsCohenRing.exists_residueField_ringEquiv`: for every prime number `p` and every field `k` of
  characteristic `p` there is a Cohen ring whose uniformizer is `p` and whose residue field is
  isomorphic to `k`.

## Implementation notes

The source's `Λ/pΛ ≅ k` is stated as the conjunction of `maximalIdeal Λ = Ideal.span {(p : Λ)}`
with a ring isomorphism `ResidueField Λ ≃+* k`. Since `IsLocalRing.ResidueField Λ` is
`Λ ⧸ maximalIdeal Λ` by definition, the two conjuncts together say exactly that `Λ/pΛ` is
isomorphic to `k`; the first is needed because `IsCohenRing` quantifies its uniformizer
existentially, so without it the second conjunct would speak of `Λ/qΛ` for an unnamed prime `q`
rather than of the `p` that is the characteristic of `k`.

The ring `Λ` is quantified over as a type in the same universe as `k`, carrying its `CommRing`,
`IsDomain` and `IsCohenRing` instances — the stronger reading, and the one the source's
construction lands in, since that construction is an `ℕ`-indexed inverse limit of quotients of a
filtered colimit of finitely generated `ℤ_p`-algebras built from `k`.

Faithfulness. (1) Both hypotheses are used: `hp` rules out `p = 0`, where the conclusion fails
because `Λ ⧸ (0) ≃ Λ` is never a field for a discrete valuation ring `Λ`, and `[CharP k p]` is
what makes `k` a candidate for `Λ/pΛ`, a ring of characteristic `p`; given `[Field k]` and
`[CharP k p]` the hypothesis `hp` may equivalently be weakened to `p ≠ 0`, and is kept in the
source's words. (2) The base is the source's: `(p : Λ)` is the image of the natural number `p`
under `ℤ → Λ`, the same prime as the characteristic of `k`, so the uniformizer is a rational
prime and not merely a prime element, and the isomorphism is one of rings, as `k` is given with
no algebra structure over anything. (3) The source names no canonical map: both `Λ` and the
isomorphism `Λ/pΛ ≅ k` are non-unique for imperfect `k`, so `Nonempty (ResidueField Λ ≃+* k)` is
the content; what the statement does pin is the ideal `pΛ` by which the quotient is formed.
(4) Not degenerate: for `k = ZMod p` the ring `Λ = ℤ_[p]` satisfies every conjunct, by
`PadicInt.maximalIdeal_eq_span_p` and `PadicInt.residueField`.

## References

* [StacksProject 0328](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0328), Lemma 10.160.6
-/

@[expose] public section

open IsLocalRing



/-- **Cohen rings exist** (Stacks 0328). Let `p` be a prime number and let `k` be a field of
characteristic `p`. Then there is a Cohen ring `Λ` whose uniformizer is `p`, i.e. with
`maximalIdeal Λ = pΛ`, and whose residue field `Λ/pΛ` is isomorphic to `k`. -/
@[stacks 0328 "The source's `Λ/pΛ ≅ k` is the conjunction of `maximalIdeal Λ = pΛ`, pinning \
  the uniformizer to the prime `p` that `IsCohenRing` (Stacks 0327) only asserts to exist, with \
  a ring isomorphism `ResidueField Λ ≃+* k`, `ResidueField Λ` being `Λ ⧸ maximalIdeal Λ`. The \
  ring `Λ` is asked for in the universe of `k`. See the module docstring.",
  ega "0 19.8.6" "(ii), special case"]
public theorem IsCohenRing.exists_residueField_ringEquiv (p : ℕ) (hp : p.Prime) (k : Type u)
    [Field k] [CharP k p] :
    ∃ (Λ : Type u) (_ : CommRing Λ) (_ : IsDomain Λ) (_ : IsCohenRing Λ),
      maximalIdeal Λ = Ideal.span {(p : Λ)} ∧ Nonempty (ResidueField Λ ≃+* k) :=
  Stacks.RingTheory.CohenRingExistence.solution p hp k

end

/-!
# Formal smoothness of `Λ/pⁿΛ` over `ℤ/pⁿℤ`

Let `Λ` be a Cohen ring whose residue field has characteristic `p`, so that `p` is a uniformizer
of `Λ`. This file proves that `ℤ/pⁿℤ → Λ/pⁿΛ` is formally smooth for every `n`, by induction on
`n`.

For `n = 0` both rings are zero. For `n = 1` the map is an extension of the finite, hence perfect,
field `𝐅_p`; every extension of a perfect field is separable, and separable extensions are
formally smooth (Stacks 0322).

The step from `n` to `n + 1`, for `n ≥ 1`, is a case of a statement about natural numbers alone:
whenever `a ∣ b` and `b ∣ a²`, formal smoothness of `ℤ/aℤ → Λ/aΛ` descends to `ℤ/bℤ → Λ/bΛ`
(`formallySmooth_of_dvd_sq`). Indeed the kernel of `ℤ/bℤ → ℤ/aℤ` is the ideal `(a)`, whose square
vanishes exactly because `b ∣ a²`, and the kernel of `Λ/bΛ → Λ/aΛ` is the ideal it generates; so
the square-zero descent of formal smoothness along a flat map (Stacks 031L, which is Mathlib's
`RingHom.FormallySmooth.of_flat_of_ker_eq_map_of_square_zero`) applies. For `a = pⁿ` and
`b = pⁿ⁺¹` the second divisibility is `n + 1 ≤ 2n`, which is why the case `n = 0` must be done by
hand.

The flatness of `ℤ/kℤ → Λ/kΛ` is base change: the prime number `p` is a nonzero non-unit of the
domain `Λ`, so `Λ` has characteristic zero, hence is a torsion-free and so flat `𝐙`-module, and
`Λ/kΛ` is `Λ ⊗_𝐙 𝐙/k𝐙`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.RingTheory.CohenFormalSmoothness.solution`: the statement of Stacks 0329, formal
  smoothness of `Λ/pⁿΛ` over `ℤ/pⁿℤ` for a Cohen ring `Λ` with residue characteristic `p`.

## Implementation notes

Completeness of `Λ` is never used. The induction is run for an arbitrary discrete valuation ring
whose maximal ideal is generated by a prime number (`formallySmooth_of_ringHom`); all that Stacks
0327 contributes is the identification of that prime with the residue characteristic.

The ideal `pⁿΛ` is written `Ideal.span {((pⁿ : ℕ) : Λ)}` throughout the induction rather than
`Ideal.span {(p : Λ) ^ n}`: the two are equal, and the first form is what makes the induction step
a statement about a pair of natural numbers, with no `n`-th power left in it. The two forms are
identified once, by `Nat.cast_pow`, at the top of `formallySmooth_of_ringHom`.

Everything is phrased for a bare ring map `ZMod (p ^ n) →+* Λ ⧸ pⁿΛ` rather than for a chosen
`Algebra` structure: a ring homomorphism out of `ZMod k` is unique, so the induction may quantify
over all of them and the statement's assumed algebra structure is one of them. In particular no
`CharP (Λ/pⁿΛ) (pⁿ)` is needed to produce the map at each stage of the induction: `ZMod.castHom`
applies with the divisor `ringChar (Λ/pⁿΛ)`, and `pⁿ = 0` there.

Four lemmas proved here are missing from Mathlib and are not tags of the Stacks Project: every field
extension of a perfect field is separable in the sense of Stacks 030O
(`Algebra.isSeparableExtension_of_perfectField`, which is Stacks 030Z; Mathlib's
`Algebra.FormallySmooth.of_perfectField` asks the extension to be essentially of finite type, which
`Λ/pΛ` over `𝐅_p` is not), a domain in which a prime number is a nonzero non-unit has
characteristic zero (`charZero_of_prime`), the kernel of `ℤ/bℤ → ℤ/aℤ` for `a ∣ b` is generated by
`a` (`ker_castHom`), and a ring map to the zero ring is formally smooth
(`formallySmooth_of_subsingleton`).

